menu "Rule engine"

    config SCRIPTING_TIME_BUDGET_MS
        int "Time budget per rule-engine event (ms, 0 = unlimited)"
        default 2000
        range 0 60000
        help
            Upper bound on how long one event may spend inside JavaScript —
            an input edge, an MQTT message, a timer, or loading a script.

            Without a bound, a user script containing an endless loop stops the
            rule engine for good: every later event, including the reload of a
            corrected script, queues up behind it and is never processed. The
            HTTP handler still answers ok, and the task watchdog only prints a
            message because CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_PANIC is not set. Since the
            script is written to NVS before it ever runs, rebooting re-enters
            the same hang.

            When the budget is exceeded, QuickJS throws InternalError at the
            next interrupt check. _run_steps() already catches it, so the rule
            fails and the engine carries on.

            2000 ms is generous for I/O automation, where a rule body is
            normally a handful of operations. Raise it if you do heavy work in
            a rule; 0 disables the check and restores the old behaviour.

endmenu
